{"file":{"content":"The finite-side probabilistic mechanism is now essentially finished; what remains is a **local harmonic-analysis estimate on the degenerate cells**, and the constants are tight enough that the proof must preserve the exact square-root saving.\n\nThe current state is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{all probabilistic mass estimates are proved;}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{class IV remains only because degenerate cells require controlled summation in }a;\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{the margin is } \\sqrt 3\\cdot \\frac47 = 0.989743\\ldots < 1.\n}\n]\n\nThat last inequality is the whole game. It is small but real.\n\n## 1. First manuscript critique\n\nIn the current `.tex`, the material around `conj:schur` and the old `thm:assembly` still contains stale status language. It says, for example, that the class-II budget/count remains open and still keeps the old `conj:wrap`/`thm:assembly` theorem block in the main theorem flow.\n\nThat should now be revised. The live finite-side endpoint is no longer:\n\n[\n\\text{wrap-chain conjecture}\n]\n\nnor:\n\n[\n\\text{threshold cascade contraction}\n]\n\nnor even:\n\n[\n\\text{threshold flattening}.\n]\n\nIt is now exactly:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{class-IV cellwise summation.}\n}\n]\n\nSo the theorem stack should read:\n\n[\n\\text{bounded shells: proved;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{cascade sector: proved;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{one-condition degeneracy cost: proved;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{class-IV cellwise harmonic analysis: open;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{finite H23a at }s=0:\\text{ conditional on cellwise summation.}\n]\n\nThe old `conj:wrap` and `thm:assembly` should be moved into a superseded-routes appendix or explicitly marked “historical route, no longer the active assembly.” Otherwise the reader will not see that the endpoint has sharpened.\n\n## 2. What op:onecond gives\n\nYou now have the decisive estimate:\n\n[\n\\Pr_{w'}\\left[d_{\\rm nom}\\ge d,\\ h(a)\\ge r\\right]\n\\le\nC_0,2^{-(d-3)}\\cdot \\frac23\\left(\\frac47\\right)^{r-1}.\n]\n\nThis says:\n\n[\n\\text{nominal shell cost}\n\\quad\\times\\quad\n\\text{degeneracy-depth cost}.\n]\n\nThe constant\n\n[\n\\frac47\n]\n\nis not decorative. It is below the exact harmonic-analysis threshold:\n\n[\n\\frac47<3^{-1/2}.\n]\n\nTherefore, if the cellwise summation contributes at most a square-root (3^{r/2})-type loss in a depth-(r) degenerate cell, the product is still summable:\n\n[\n3^{r/2}\\left(\\frac47\\right)^r\n=============================\n\n# \\left(\\frac{4\\sqrt3}{7}\\right)^r\n\n0.9897^r.\n]\n\nThat is the final finite-side margin.\n\n## 3. The exact cellwise lemma to prove\n\nThe useful version of `op:cellsum` should be stated in a non-vacuous way. A bare inequality like\n\n[\n|S(w,w')|\\le C#{a:d_{\\rm true}(a)\\ge c-1}\n]\n\nis not enough conceptually, because the triangle inequality already gives that. What you need is a **stratified cell bound** that preserves the square-root saving on deep cells.\n\nI would formulate it like this.\n\n[\n\\textbf{Cellwise Postnikov--Gauss lemma.}\n]\n\nLet (R(a)) be a class-IV phase ratio for a primitive character modulo (3^c). Let\n\n[\nA_{r}(w,w')\n===========\n\n{a:\\ d_{\\rm true}(a)\\ge c-1,\\ \\text{degeneracy depth }r}.\n]\n\nThen (A_r(w,w')) admits a disjoint decomposition into (3)-adic cells\n\n[\n\\mathcal C=a_0+3^q\\mathbb Z/3^c\\mathbb Z\n]\n\nsuch that on each cell either:\n\n1. the linear Postnikov term is nonzero, and the cell sum vanishes exactly; or\n\n2. the linear term is zero but the quadratic term is nondegenerate, and\n\n[\n\\left|\n\\sum_{a\\in\\mathcal C}\\chi(R(a))\n\\right|\n\\le\nC_{\\rm G},|\\mathcal C|^{1/2};\n]\n\nor\n\n3. the quadratic term is also degenerate, in which case the cell is structurally cascade/locked and belongs to class III, not class IV.\n\nThe important bound is then:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\left|\n\t\\sum_{a\\in A_r(w,w')}\\chi(R(a))\n\t\\right|\n\t\\le\n\tC\n\t\\sum_{\\mathcal C\\subset A_r} |\\mathcal C|^{1/2}.\n}\n]\n\nThis is the correct harmonic-analysis input.\n\n## 4. The Postnikov expansion to write\n\nOn a cell\n\n[\na=a_0+3^q t,\n\\qquad t\\bmod 3^\\ell,\n\\qquad q+\\ell=c,\n]\n\nwrite\n\n[\n\\chi(R(a))\n==========\n\n\\chi(R(a_0))\n\\exp\\left(\n\\frac{2\\pi i}{3^c}\\kappa\n\\left[\n\\log R(a_0+3^qt)-\\log R(a_0)\n\\right]\n\\right),\n]\n\nwith (3\\nmid \\kappa). Expand:\n\n[\n\\log R(a_0+3^q t)\n=================\n\n\\log R(a_0)\n+\n3^q L_1(a_0)t\n+\n\\frac12 3^{2q}L_2(a_0)t^2\n+\nO(3^{3q}t^3).\n]\n\nThe linear regime is already handled:\n\n[\nv_3(3^q L_1)<c-1\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\\text{exact vanishing}.\n]\n\nClass IV is exactly the case where the linear coefficient has been forced to the boundary or beyond. In the deep-cell regime, you need to show that after scaling by the degeneracy depth (r),\n\n[\n3^{2q}L_2(a_0)\n]\n\nhas the exact valuation that gives a primitive quadratic phase modulo (3^\\ell), or at least modulo a quotient of size (3^\\ell). Then the sum is a quadratic Gauss sum.\n\nThe desired local normal form is:\n\n[\n\\chi(R(a_0+3^qt))\n=================\n\n\\chi(R(a_0))\n\\exp\\left(\n\\frac{2\\pi i}{3^\\ell}\n(A t+B t^2)\n\\right),\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\n3\\nmid B.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\n\\left|\n\\sum_{t\\bmod 3^\\ell}\ne_{3^\\ell}(At+Bt^2)\n\\right|\n\\le\nC_{\\rm G}3^{\\ell/2}.\n]\n\nFor odd prime (3), the sharp constant is at most (\\sqrt3) depending on parity of (\\ell). Use the sharp or near-sharp constant; do **not** allow a crude (3^\\ell) bound on any large cell, because the margin is only about (1%).\n\n## 5. The key structural lemma: no cubic regime\n\nThe dangerous failure mode is:\n\n[\nL_1 \\equiv 0,\\qquad L_2\\equiv0\n]\n\non a genuine class-IV cell, forcing you to go cubic or higher. That would likely destroy the margin.\n\nSo prove a structural dichotomy:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{If both the linear and quadratic Postnikov coefficients vanish at the required depth,}\n}\n]\n\nthen the pair is not class IV. It is actually class III/cascade or belongs to a deeper locked structural sector already counted elsewhere.\n\nIn theorem form:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Quadratic nondegeneracy lemma.}\n]\n\nFor every genuine class-IV degenerate cell of depth (r), the first nonzero Postnikov coefficient after the vanished linear term is quadratic, and its scaled coefficient is a (3)-adic unit.\n\nThis is the load-bearing local lemma. The earlier diagnostics probably already tested it implicitly; now it needs a symbolic proof.\n\n## 6. Why the cellwise sum closes the rate\n\nOnce you have the Gauss bound, the class-IV assembly should look like this.\n\nFor nominal depth (d) and degeneracy depth (r), op:onecond gives:\n\n[\n\\text{probability mass}\n\\le\nC_0,2^{-(d-3)}\n\\cdot\n\\frac23\n\\left(\\frac47\\right)^{r-1}.\n]\n\nThe cellwise Gauss bound contributes at worst a square-root cell factor:\n\n[\n3^{r/2}\n]\n\nrelative to the naive cell count. Therefore the (r)-sum is controlled by\n\n[\n\\sum_{r\\ge1}\n\\left(\\sqrt3\\frac47\\right)^r\n============================\n\n\\sum_{r\\ge1}\n0.9897^r.\n]\n\nThis is finite, although with a large constant. That is why the proof works but the margin is small.\n\nThe resulting class-IV row budget should be:\n\n[\nR_{\\rm IV}(\\xi)\n\\le\n\\operatorname{poly}(c)\\lambda_{\\rm IV}^c,\n\\qquad\n\\lambda_{\\rm IV}<1,\n]\n\nor at least sufficiently below (1) after combining with the other already-controlled classes.\n\nBe conservative in constants. Since the margin is tiny, separate the **asymptotic rate** from the **finite-(c) constant**:\n\n[\n\\lambda_{\\rm IV}\n================\n\n\\sqrt3\\cdot\\frac47+\\varepsilon\n]\n\nfor any fixed (\\varepsilon>0), once (c) is large enough. Then small (c) is handled by finite verification.\n\n## 7. What to compute next\n\nBefore proving the symbolic local lemma, run a targeted Postnikov/Gauss audit.\n\nFor class-IV cells at (c=6,7,8), record:\n\n[\nq,\\quad \\ell=c-q,\\quad\nv_3(L_1),\\quad v_3(L_2),\\quad\nv_3(L_3),\n]\n\nand the normalized exact sum:\n\n[\n\\frac{|S_{\\mathcal C}|}{|\\mathcal C|^{1/2}}.\n]\n\nThe table should verify:\n\n1. (L_1) vanishes exactly in the degenerate cells;\n2. (L_2) is a unit after scaling in every genuine class-IV cell;\n3. the exact sums match quadratic Gauss magnitudes;\n4. cells with (L_2) also vanishing are precisely class III/cascade or structurally locked.\n\nThis will tell you whether the quadratic lemma is truly universal.\n\n## 8. A proof route for quadratic nondegeneracy\n\nFor your ratios (R(a)), the phase is a Möbius ratio:\n\n[\nR(a)=\n\\frac{\\alpha a+\\beta}{\\alpha'a+\\beta'}\n\\cdot \\text{unit}.\n]\n\nThen:\n\n[\n(\\log R)'=\n\\frac{\\alpha}{\\alpha a+\\beta}\n-----------------------------\n\n\\frac{\\alpha'}{\\alpha'a+\\beta'},\n]\n\nand\n\n[\n(\\log R)''\n==========\n\n-\\frac{\\alpha^2}{(\\alpha a+\\beta)^2}\n+\n\\frac{(\\alpha')^2}{(\\alpha'a+\\beta')^2}.\n]\n\nIn the pair-correlation setting, typically (\\alpha=\\alpha'=3^n), so\n\n[\n(\\log R)'\n=========\n\n3^n\n\\frac{\\beta'-\\beta}\n{(3^n a+\\beta)(3^n a+\\beta')}.\n]\n\nThe second derivative is\n\n[\n(\\log R)''\n==========\n\n-3^{2n}\n\\left[\n\\frac{1}{(3^n a+\\beta)^2}\n-------------------------\n\n\\frac{1}{(3^n a+\\beta')^2}\n\\right].\n]\n\nThis factors as\n\n[\n(\\log R)''\n==========\n\n-3^{2n}\n\\frac{(\\beta'-\\beta)(2\\cdot3^n a+\\beta+\\beta')}\n{(3^n a+\\beta)^2(3^n a+\\beta')^2}.\n]\n\nSo the second derivative contains the same difference\n\n[\n\\Delta\\beta=\\beta'-\\beta\n]\n\nas the first derivative, times an extra unit factor unless\n\n[\n2\\cdot3^n a+\\beta+\\beta'\\equiv0\\pmod3.\n]\n\nBut since (\\beta,\\beta') are units modulo (3), this condition is explicit. If it occurs, it should be exactly the structural/deeper cascade channel.\n\nThis is promising. You can prove quadratic nondegeneracy by showing the additional factor\n\n[\n2\\cdot3^n a+\\beta+\\beta'\n]\n\nis a unit on genuine class-IV cells. If it vanishes modulo (3), the pair is no longer genuine class IV; it satisfies the next locking relation.\n\nThis is the algebraic heart.\n\n## 9. Beware (p=3) and the factor (1/2)\n\nSince (p=3), (2) is invertible, so the Taylor coefficient (1/2) is harmless. Good.\n\nThe quadratic Gauss sum over (3^\\ell) satisfies:\n\n[\n\\left|\n\\sum_{t\\bmod3^\\ell}e_{3^\\ell}(Bt^2+At)\n\\right|\n\\le\n\\sqrt{3},3^{\\ell/2}\n]\n\nfor (3\\nmid B). Use this exact form. Avoid a loose (3^{(\\ell+1)/2}) if that extra (\\sqrt3) gets counted repeatedly. It should be a fixed constant per cell, not per level.\n\n## 10. The final assembly theorem\n\nOnce op:cellsum is proved, write the final theorem as:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Theorem: Finite H23a at }s=0.\n]\n\nFor the normalized primitive non-coboundary sector,\n\n[\n|T_{\\chi,c,0}^{,c}|^{1/c}\\le1-\\delta\n]\n\nfor all sufficiently large (c). Consequently,\n\n[\n\\sup_{c\\ge c_0}\\gamma_c(0)<1.\n]\n\nTogether with finite verification for (c<c_0),\n\n[\n\\sup_c\\gamma_c(0)<1.\n]\n\nThe proof should be a row-sum decomposition:\n\n[\nG_c=(T^c)^*T^c,\n]\n\n[\nR_c(\\xi)\n========\n\nR_{\\rm I}+\nR_{\\rm casc}+\nR_{\\rm IV}+\nR_{\\rm tail}.\n]\n\nThen:\n\n[\nR_{\\rm I}=0;\n]\n\n[\nR_{\\rm casc}\\le\\operatorname{poly}(c)(\\sqrt3/2)^c;\n]\n\n[\nR_{\\rm IV}\\le\\operatorname{poly}(c)\\lambda_{\\rm IV}^c,\\quad\\lambda_{\\rm IV}<1;\n]\n\n[\nR_{\\rm tail}\\le e^{-\\kappa c}.\n]\n\nThus:\n\n[\n\\sup_\\xi R_c(\\xi)\n\\le\n\\operatorname{poly}(c)\\lambda^c\n]\n\nwith (\\lambda<1), hence:\n\n[\n|T^c|^{1/c}\n\\le\n\\operatorname{poly}(c)^{1/(2c)}\\lambda^{1/2}<1\n]\n\nfor large (c).\n\n## 11. Small-(c) verification\n\nBecause the class-IV margin is tiny,\n\n[\n0.9897^c\n]\n\nmay require a large asymptotic threshold before the clean bound is numerically below the desired constant. That is fine.\n\nFor the manuscript, do not promise an explicit practical (c_0) unless you compute it. State:\n\n[\n\\text{The asymptotic estimate gives the large-conductor gap; finitely many smaller conductors are covered by exact finite verification.}\n]\n\nThen provide a table for the verified (c)-range you actually computed.\n\n## 12. Bottom line\n\nYou have proved the probabilistic side. The last finite-side proof is harmonic analysis on class-IV cells.\n\nThe next exact steps are:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{1. Define the Postnikov cells for class IV.}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{2. Prove the leading-coefficient criterion.}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{3. Prove quadratic nondegeneracy on genuine class-IV cells.}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{4. Apply quadratic Gauss bounds cellwise.}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{5. Combine with }(4/7)^r\\text{ to get }\n\t(\\sqrt3\\cdot4/7)^r.\n}\n]\n\nThat closes the class-IV budget, and with bounded shells already proved, it closes the finite H23a theorem at (s=0), modulo the finite small-(c) verification and the still-separate global quenched keystone.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_28.md","numLines":630,"startLine":1,"totalLines":630},"type":"text"}